From: kkw537 <171009921+kkw537@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: t6000-j314s: boot the PMP

The Power Management Processor's driver (CONFIG_APPLE_PMP, pmp.ko) is
built, but its node is disabled, so the PMP is never booted and its
power reports go unread. The Asahi Linux 7.0 progress report measured
about half a watt (some 20 %) less at idle on a 14-inch M1 Pro with it
on, and leaves it off by default until the driver is upstream; this is
the opt-in it describes: define APPLE_USE_PMP before the SoC includes,
which routes the DCP, DCPext, ISP and AVD power domains through the PMP
report entries, and enable the PMP node. The j314/j316 board file
already enables the ISP report entry that the ISP then uses.

Only the 14-inch M1 Pro (J314s) board is changed. The compiled DTB
differs from the stock one only in the pmp alias, the PMP node's status
and those power-domain references.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6000-j314s.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6000-j314s.dts
@@ -9,6 +9,9 @@
 
 /dts-v1/;
 
+/* Boot the PMP and route the DCP, ISP and AVD power domains through it. */
+#define APPLE_USE_PMP
+
 #include "t6000.dtsi"
 #include "t600x-j314-j316.dtsi"
 
@@ -41,3 +44,7 @@
 	compatible = "apple,j314-macaudio", "apple,macaudio";
 	model = "MacBook Pro J314";
 };
+
+&pmp {
+	status = "okay";
+};
